# Battery-Resistor Circuit

## Objective

Read a formula-driven circuit schematic and distinguish an animated direction
indicator from the constant current value stated by the example.

## Prerequisites

This is a standalone reference, not a prerequisite in the guided sequence.
Lesson [08](./08_Formula_Driven_Diagrams.amx) introduces formula-driven
diagrams and their time controls.

## Desktop workflow

Open this document in OpenAMX Desktop. Select **Run** to inspect the
calculated voltage, resistance, and current; select **Preview** to inspect
the schematic and its playback controls. Scrubbing changes the arrow's
position on the wire.

## Inputs and support files

This reference is input-free and imports no modules.

## Circuit schematic

```amx
let supplyVoltage: Number = 9
let resistance: Number = 15
let current: Number = supplyVoltage / resistance
fn indicatorX(t: Number): Number = 58 + 25 * t
diagram batteryResistor {
  title: "Battery-resistor DC circuit"
  description: "A 9 volt battery drives a 15 ohm resistor; the arrow travels clockwise to indicate conventional current."
  width: 320
  height: 200
  time t from 0 to 4 duration 4
  line upperLeftWire {
    x1: 50
    y1: 130
    x2: 188
    y2: 130
    stroke: "#52616B"
    lineWidth: 3
    arrow: "none"
  }
  line upperRightWire {
    x1: 218
    y1: 130
    x2: 250
    y2: 130
    stroke: "#52616B"
    lineWidth: 3
    arrow: "none"
  }
  line leftUpperWire {
    x1: 50
    y1: 130
    x2: 50
    y2: 98
    stroke: "#52616B"
    lineWidth: 3
    arrow: "none"
  }
  line leftLowerWire {
    x1: 50
    y1: 68
    x2: 50
    y2: 50
    stroke: "#52616B"
    lineWidth: 3
    arrow: "none"
  }
  line rightWire {
    x1: 250
    y1: 130
    x2: 250
    y2: 50
    stroke: "#52616B"
    lineWidth: 3
    arrow: "none"
  }
  line lowerWire {
    x1: 250
    y1: 50
    x2: 50
    y2: 50
    stroke: "#52616B"
    lineWidth: 3
    arrow: "none"
  }
  line positivePlate {
    x1: 39
    y1: 92
    x2: 61
    y2: 92
    stroke: "#263238"
    lineWidth: 4
    arrow: "none"
  }
  line negativePlate {
    x1: 43
    y1: 74
    x2: 57
    y2: 74
    stroke: "#263238"
    lineWidth: 3
    arrow: "none"
  }
  rectangle resistor {
    x: 188
    y: 118
    width: 30
    height: 24
    stroke: "#263238"
    fill: "#FFF5E6"
    lineWidth: 2
  }
  line currentIndicator {
    x1: indicatorX(t)
    y1: 130
    x2: indicatorX(t) + 14
    y2: 130
    stroke: "#D04A3A"
    lineWidth: 4
    arrow: "end"
  }
  text voltageLabel {
    x: 20
    y: 24
    value: "9 V"
    fill: "#263238"
  }
  text resistanceLabel {
    x: 180
    y: 156
    value: "15 ohm"
    fill: "#263238"
  }
  text currentLabel {
    x: 88
    y: 154
    value: "I = ${round(current, 2)} A"
    fill: "#263238"
  }
}
```

## Expected outcome and caveat

The displayed values are a `9 V` source, a `15 ohm` resistor, and the
arithmetic result `0.6 A`. The current magnitude is constant in this example.
Only the red arrow's position changes with diagram-local time to indicate
conventional-current direction; its motion does not represent changing
current magnitude.

This is an illustrative schematic, not an engineering circuit model. Diagram
coordinates are schematic coordinates, not physical axes or measured units.
HTML and desktop Preview provide interactive controls. PDF and DOCX contain
only the static frame at the lower time bound; DOCX uses an image, not
editable native Word shapes.

